<div>
<p>This lesson walks through the key ideas of the unit and then applies them to three works. Start with the Watch &amp; Listen strip above, then read on.</p>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    Television and Cinema: Similarities and Differences
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Television and cinema both use the moving image, but they work differently. Cinema is designed for concentrated viewing in a dark room. Television was designed for the living room, with interruptions, commercial breaks, and the expectation that the viewer might be doing something else at the same time. These conditions shaped the art form. Television developed structures (the self-contained episode, the recurring cast, the cliffhanger ending) that cinema does not need.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    The Television Series and Serial
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>This lesson distinguishes between the <strong>episodic series</strong> (each episode is self-contained) and the <strong>serial</strong> (a continuous story that unfolds across many episodes or seasons). The serial form has become increasingly important in what critics call "prestige television" or "the golden age of television." Shows like <em>The Sopranos</em>, <em>The Wire</em>, and <em>Downton Abbey</em> use the serial's extended time frame to develop character and theme with a depth that a two-hour film cannot achieve.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Video Art
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p><strong>Video art</strong> uses the moving image for non-commercial, often experimental purposes. It typically avoids narrative. Artists like Nam June Paik, Bill Viola, and Magdalena Fernández use video to create installations, meditations on time and perception, or abstract visual experiences. Video art is closer to painting and sculpture in its goals than it is to television, even though it uses the same technology.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">4</span>
    Television as a Medium: Schedule, Format, and the Living Room
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#9660;</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Television is shaped by where and how it is watched. Early broadcast television reached everyone in a household at the same hour, which created shared experiences: a comedy everyone discussed the next day, a news report that told a country what had happened. Shows were built for interruption, with act breaks for commercials, so each segment had to end on a hook. Sitcoms such as <em>I Love Lucy</em> used three cameras and a live audience to capture comedy as a performance, and their tidy half-hour structure (a problem, escalating complications, resolution) suited a living-room schedule.</p>
<p>Streaming and recording have changed the conditions. Viewers can watch several episodes at once, and writers can plan whole seasons as single stories. Ask of any television work: who is it made for, in what setting, and how does the format shape the story it can tell? Form and context are inseparable here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">5</span>
    Video Art: Looping, Installation, and Time
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#9660;</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Video art grew out of artists' interest in a new technology in the 1960s. Nam June Paik, a Korean-born artist often called the father of video art, manipulated television signals with magnets and mixed music, dance, and found footage; his <em>Global Groove</em> (1973) imagined a future in which viewers could tune in to the whole world's channels. His <em>TV Buddha</em> turned the camera on a statue and let it watch its own image.</p>
<p>Video art is usually shown in a gallery, often as an <strong>installation</strong>: screens placed in a dark room so that the viewer's body is part of the experience. Many works <strong>loop</strong>, running without a beginning or end, so you can enter at any point and stay as long as you like. That contradicts the structure of a film or a television episode, which has a fixed running time. When you look at a video work, ask where it is shown, how long you are expected to stay, and what it asks you to do with your body and your attention.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">6</span>
    Writing About Television: Pilot, Season, and Genre
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#9660;</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Television is often written about as if each show were one thing, but its structure has several layers. The <strong>pilot</strong> is the first episode, which has to introduce the world, the central characters, and the question that will keep viewers returning. The <strong>episode arc</strong> is the story told within a single hour or half hour. The <strong>season arc</strong> is a larger shape that stretches across many episodes, and the <strong>series arc</strong> is the largest, spanning the whole run. A serial such as <em>The Wire</em> plays most strongly on the larger arcs; a sitcom such as <em>I Love Lucy</em> plays mostly on the episode arc.</p>
<p><strong>Genre</strong> shapes expectations: a police procedural promises a case solved by the end of the hour, a sitcom promises laughter and a return to normal, and a prestige drama may promise neither. Strong television often uses genre and then bends it. Television is also a writers' medium in a way that cinema is not: a head writer, often called the <strong>showrunner</strong>, usually guides the whole series, and a team of writers builds its stories. For that reason, critics often discuss a series' writing and structure first, and its performances and visual style after.</p>
<p>When you write about a television work, say which layer you are discussing (an episode, a season, a whole series), which genre it works within, and how it bends that genre's expectations. These choices will make your description precise.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</div><hr>
<h3>Supplemental Works</h3>
<p>The works below expand on the concepts you just learned. Each one connects to a concept from the lesson and gives you a chance to practice the kind of thinking this unit introduces.</p>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    Bill Viola, The Crossing (1996)
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<div class="video-embed"><iframe title="Bill Viola, The Crossing (1996)" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/fHqhaH6m9pY?si=iYyBi9UMRqbL1hdU" width="560" height="315" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
<p>Connects to: Video Art / Participation / Time-Based Art</p>
<p>Viola's <em>The Crossing</em> is a two-channel video installation: on one screen, a man walks toward the viewer and is consumed by fire from below; on the other, the same man is consumed by water from above. Both events play in extreme slow motion over about twelve minutes. The slowness transforms what would be a brief, violent event into a sustained meditation on transformation, destruction, and renewal.</p>
<div class="callout callout--experience"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">👁️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Try this</p>
<p>Video art is time-based in a way that painting is not. You cannot see <em>The Crossing</em> "all at once"; you must stay with it as it unfolds. How does this change the nature of participation compared to standing in front of a painting? Does the slow pace make participation easier or harder?</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    The Wire, Season 1 (2002)
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<div class="video-embed"><iframe title="The Wire, Season 1 (2002)" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/uDcQbk78CSw?si=c4kxfXFtEx0jGcxY" width="560" height="315" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
<p>Connects to: The Television Serial / Character / Form-Content</p>
<p>David Simon's <em>The Wire</em> examines the Baltimore drug trade from multiple perspectives simultaneously: police, drug dealers, addicts, politicians, educators, and journalists. Its serial structure allows it to develop dozens of characters over five seasons, each with their own arc. The show resists the satisfying resolutions of episodic television; cases go unsolved, reforms fail, and characters who seem central are sidelined or killed. The form mirrors the content: the institutions depicted in the show (police bureaucracy, the drug economy, city government) are self-perpetuating systems that resist individual efforts to change them.</p>
<div class="callout callout--experience"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">👁️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Try this</p>
<p>This lesson asks whether certain television shows qualify as a serious art form. Apply the three evaluative standards from Unit 3 (perfection, insight, inexhaustibility) to <em>The Wire</em> or another television series you know well. Does it meet those standards? Where does it fall short?</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Nam June Paik, TV Buddha (1974)
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<div class="video-embed"><iframe title="Nam June Paik, TV Buddha (1974)" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/MVwR1n0IZAs?si=3smwXEgrlAAUM426" width="560" height="315" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
<p>Connects to: Video Art / Conceptual Art / Participation</p>
<p>Paik placed a Buddha statue in front of a closed-circuit television camera connected to a monitor. The Buddha "watches" its own live image on the screen in an endless feedback loop. There is no narrative, no editing, no progression. The work simply exists as a situation: a contemplative figure contemplating its own electronic image.</p>
<div class="callout callout--experience"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">👁️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Try this</p>
<p>Is <em>TV Buddha</em> a sculpture, a video work, or a conceptual piece? This lesson's genre categories (painting, sculpture, cinema, television) may not accommodate works like this cleanly. What does that tell you about the limits of genre-based analysis?</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">4</span>
    Looking Ahead
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>This is the last unit of the course. Over thirteen units, you have built a vocabulary and a set of critical tools for analyzing painting, sculpture, architecture, literature, theater, music, dance, photography, cinema, and television/video art. The final exam will test your ability to apply these tools across all the art forms. The fundamental questions have stayed the same throughout: what values does this work reveal, and how does its form make those values visible?</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</div></div>
</div>

<div class="callout callout--experience"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128065;&#65039;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Experience It: Watching Time</strong></p>
<p>Choose one work from Watch &amp; Listen above and give it at least five uninterrupted minutes. Put your phone away.</p>
<ol>
<li>What changes on the screen during your viewing, and what stays the same?</li>
<li>How does the work use time: fast, slow, repeated, or looping? What does that do to your attention?</li>
<li>Could you have taken in this work in a single glance, as you can with a painting? What does its time-based form let it do?</li>
</ol>
</div></div>

